\title{rddid: treatment effects at a cutoff shared with a confounding policy}
\description{
A treatment of interest switches on at a cutoff of a running variable in one
period, the \strong{RD period}. A \strong{confounding policy} switches at the same
cutoff, in every period, so the jump in the outcome at the cutoff in the RD
period mixes the treatment effect with the \strong{confounding jump}. In the
\strong{comparison periods} the treatment of interest is uniform at the cutoff
(nobody treated, or everybody treated), so the jump there \emph{is} the
confounding jump. \code{\link[=rddid]{rddid()}} estimates the jump in every period by
local-linear RD and subtracts a weighted average of the comparison-period
jumps from the RD-period jump. How the weights are set is the
\strong{confounding-trend assumption}: constant (equal weights) or linear in time.
}
\section{Workflow}{

\enumerate{
\item \strong{Estimate.} \code{fit <- rddid(data, y = , x = , time = , id = , t_rd = )};
printing \code{fit} shows the estimate, its standard error and confidence
interval, the comparison periods with their weights, the sampling scheme
and the bandwidth. See \code{\link[=rddid]{rddid()}}.
\item \strong{Look inside.} \code{summary(fit)} shows the jump in every period and the
standard error under each sampling scheme (\code{\link[=summary.rddid]{summary.rddid()}}); \code{coef()},
\code{confint()} and \code{nobs()} work as usual (\link{rddid-methods}), and \code{tidy()}
and \code{glance()} feed table makers (\link{rddid-tidiers}).
\item \strong{Check the assumptions.} When the running variable moves over time,
units can change side of the cutoff between periods, and four tests check
the assumptions this adds: type continuity (\code{\link[=rd_typecont]{rd_typecont()}}), composition
stability (\code{\link[=rd_compstable]{rd_compstable()}}), homogeneous confounding (\code{\link[=rd_homog]{rd_homog()}}) and
constant within-type confounding (\code{\link[=rd_trendcell]{rd_trendcell()}}).
}

The building blocks \code{\link[=rd_period]{rd_period()}} (the local-linear RD in one period) and
\code{\link[=rd_bw_cct]{rd_bw_cct()}} (its CCT bandwidths) are exported for users who want to work
period by period. \link{rddid_sim} and \link{rddid_sim_pv} are simulated panels for
trying things out. \code{citation("rddid")} gives the reference below.
}

